# Illumination Enhanced Crystallization and Defect Passivation for High Performance CsPbI3 Perovskite Solar Cells by Sacrificing Dye

## abstract
All‐inorganic perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have been the research focus due to their high thermal stability and proper band gap for tandem solar cells. However, their power conversion efficiency (PCE) is still lower than that of organic‐inorganic hybrid PSCs. Herein, a sacrificing dye (Rhodamine B isothiocyanate, RBITC) is developed to regulate the growth of perovskite film by in situ release of ethylammonium cations, isothiocyanate anions and benzoic acid molecules upon annealing and illumination. The ethylammonium cations can efficiently passivate surface defects. The isothiocyanate anions incorporate with uncoordinated Pb to regulate the crystallization process. The benzoic acid molecules facilitate the nucleation of the perovskite crystals. Especially, the illumination can accelerate the release of these beneficial ions/molecules to improve the quality of perovskite films further. After optimization with RBITC, a high open circuit voltage (VOC) of 1.24 V and a champion PCE of 20.95% are obtained, which are among the highest Voc and PCE values of CsPbI3 PSCs. Accordingly, the operational stability of the PSC devices is significantly improved. The results provide an efficient chemical strategy to regulate the formation of perovskite films in whole crystallization process for high performance all‐inorganic PSCs.

All-inorganic perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have been the research focus due to their high thermal stability and proper band gap for tandem solar cells. However, their power conversion efficiency (PCE) is still lower than that of organic-inorganic hybrid PSCs. Herein, a sacrificing dye (Rhodamine B isothiocyanate, RBITC) is developed to regulate the growth of perovskite film by in situ release of ethylammonium cations, isothiocyanate anions and benzoic acid molecules upon annealing and illumination. The ethylammonium cations can efficiently passivate surface defects. The isothiocyanate anions incorporate with uncoordinated Pb to regulate the crystallization process. The benzoic acid molecules facilitate the nucleation of the perovskite crystals. Especially, the illumination can accelerate the release of these beneficial ions/molecules to improve the quality of perovskite films further. After optimization with RBITC, a high open circuit voltage $(\pmb{V}_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of 1.24 V and a champion PCE of $20.95\%$ are obtained, which are among the highest Voc and PCE values of $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}$ PSCs. Accordingly, the operational stability of the PSC devices is significantly improved. The results provide an efficient chemical strategy to regulate the formation of perovskite films in whole crystallization process for high performance all-inorganic PSCs.
## 1. Introduction
Endowed by high thermal stability, rapidly increased efficiency, all-inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have attracted great attention in recent years. Before 2020, the researches on the all-inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ perovskite solar cells (PSCs) emerge and the reported power conversion efficiency (PCE) increased fast from $5.7\%$ to $17.3\%$ , while the PCE was much lower than $20\%$ .[1–6] After 2020, the PCE broke $20\%$ successfully, which makes the research of all-inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ PSCs an active research field.[7–20] Recently, the PCEs of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ further stepped up to the plateau of $21\%$ .[21–23] In addition, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ perovskite has a suitable bandgap $(1.7~\mathrm{eV})$ to combine with silicon solar cells for tandem device with a theoretical efficiency of $44\%$ .[24]
However, the PCE of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ PSCs is still much lower than that of organicinorganic hybrid PSCs due to the serious open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ loss.[25] The $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss is defined as the difference of VOC and band gap, i.e. $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ $\mathrm{loss}=\mathrm{E}g/e\mathrm{-}V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ where $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Eg}}$ is the band gap of perovskite film and $e$ is the elementary charge. For the state-of-art organic-inorganic hybrid PSCs $25.6\%$ PCE), the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss is only 0.341 V, with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.189\mathrm{~V~}$ for a band gap of $1.53\ \mathrm{eV}.^{[26]}$ In contrast, the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss is as high as $0.466\mathrm{V}$ for the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ PSCs with the highest PCE $(21.15\%)$ . $^{[23]}V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss has been a figure of merit to evaluate the quality of PSC devices.[7,16,22] Recently, a small $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss of $0.47\mathrm{V}$ was reported by Che et al. using benzoyl hydrazine as additive to regulate the perovskite growth process.[19] $\mathtt{X u}$ et al. obtained a rather small $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss of $0.451\mathrm{~V~}$ and a high PCE of $20.89\%$ by treating the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface with 3-sulphonatopropyl acrylate potassium.[16] Wang et al. reported the use of 1,2-Di(thiophen2-yl)ethane-1,2-dione as an additive to regulate the growth of perovskite film and a PCE of $21.15\%$ was reported.[23] In these works, the key to reduce $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss is to regulate the perovskite film growth and passivate the interface. The goal is to decrease the defect density at the grain boundaries and surface of perovskite film.
Additive engineering is an efficient way to regulate the formation process of perovskite film.[27] The dominate defects in perovskite film responsible to the degradation of PSCs is iodine vacancies $(V_{\mathrm{I}})$ ,[28,29] which results in the localization of both electrons and holes.[30] It has been proved that the stronger binder to Pb will increase the formation energy of the adjacent $V_{\mathrm{I}}$ .[19,31] So the ions with strong coordination binding energy with $\mathrm{\DeltaPb}$ are ideal to play an additive role in the formation of perovskite film. Wu et al. used a volatile organic molecule (glycolic acid) to regulate the perovskite crystallinity by the coordination interaction between $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ and $\mathrm{Pb}$ , while the nonvolatile additive (thioglycolic acid) will damage electronic properties of perovskite film.[32] Isothiocyanate salts are also used as additive to regulate the formation of perovskite film.[33] Che et al. used benzoyl hydrazine as additive in precursor solution to the eliminate $V_{\mathrm{I}}$ defects, and a PCE of $20.47\%$ was achieved, where $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ and - ${\mathrm{NH}}{=}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups interact strongly with undercoordinate Pb.[19] In this work, though the molecules are not volatile under annealing due to their relative large size, they accumulate at the surface of crystal grains to passivate the defects on the surfaces, which will not deteriorate the electronic properties.[34,35] As such, volatile small molecules and non-volatile larger molecules with strong interaction with $\mathrm{{Pb}}$ are gradually applied to regulate the formation of perovskite film.
In previous researches, the functional additives are usually directly added into perovskite precursor solutions.[27] Those additives interact with the perovskite components during stirring for a long time before coating. If volatile additives are used, their content in the precursor solution may decrease due to their volatility, which compromises their effect. For nonvolatile additives, their strong binding with Pb may lead to the decrease of grain size, which is undesired to high performance PSCs.[36] Herein, we introduce a sacrificing dye (Rhodamine B isothiocyanate, RBITC) as an additive to regulate the formation of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ perovskite film. The illumination sensitive RBITC cations decompose completely due to the efficient catalytic degradation by the newly formed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ nanocrystals (NCs) upon thermal annealing and the effect is enhanced by illumination. Benzoic acid (BA) molecules, isothiocyanate $(\mathsf{S C N^{-}})$ anions, and ethylammonium $(\mathrm{EA}^{+})$ cations are released to in situ interact with the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ NCs precursor solutions. The carboxylic acid group in BA molecules promote the initial crystallization of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ NCs. The $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ anions can deter the process of crystallization and increase the crystallinity of perovskite film.[37] The $\mathrm{EA^{+}}$ cations regulate the growth of perovskite film,[38] effectively passivate the defects at the perovskite grain boundaries and surfaces, and stabilize the phase of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ in photoactive black phase. Under illumination, more beneficial small ions can be released promptly to further improve the quality of the perovskite film. Based on the optimized $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ perovskite film, the champion PSC device delivers a high PCE of $20.95\%$ . The enhancement of PCE is mainly due to the increase of $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and fill factor (FF), which is consistent with the increased quality of perovskite film and better charge transportation caused by the reduced energy band offset. The $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss is as low as $0.446\mathrm{V},$ which is the lowest among the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ solar cells with PCE over $19\%$ to our best knowledge (Table S1, Supporting Information). Additionally, the PCE of the unencapsulated PSC devices remain $90.39\%$ of the initial efficiency after storing for $1000\mathrm{~h~}$ in $25\%$ humid condition, and $86.02\%$ of the initial efficiency after storing for $500\mathrm{~h~}$ in $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which manifests the high stability of RBITC optimized PSCs. Our strategy of in situ regulation of the perovskite film formation and defect passivation by illumination enhanced thermal-photon catalytic degradation of a large organic molecules provides an efficient way to achieve high performance all-inorganic PSCs in a new dimension. In this strategy, functional groups may construct a large molecule to be catalytically degraded by $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ NCs during annealing under heat and illumination to facilitate the formation of the perovskite crystal grains and defect passivation and many other possible functions. The superior catalysis performance of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ NCs also portends their enormous potential in photocatalytic chemistry.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The molecular structure of RBITC is shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information). The main active groups of RBITC are the carboxylic acid groups, isothiocyanate groups and the charged amine groups, which can interact with perovskite components. The carboxylic acid and isothiocyanate groups are supposed to interact with unoccupied electron orbitals of Pb atoms in perovskite component for their existence of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ and $\mathrm{C=}\mathrm{S}$ bonds.[31,32,37] However, for $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ perovskite precursor solutions with RBITC, the main interactions were found only to occur between RBITC and iodide anions. From the X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS), the addition of RBITC to $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ solution only induces slightly $\mathrm{Pb}$ 4f peak shift (0.05 and $0.11\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{\sfPb}$ 4f 5/2 and $\mathrm{Pb~}4\mathrm{f}~7/2$ , respectively) to lower energy (Figure S2, Supporting Information), while the I 3d peaks shift $(0.61\mathrm{eV})$ to lower energy. The same phenomena were observed for CsI solution with RBITC additives. The $\mathrm{Cs}3\mathrm{d}$ peaks shift slightly to higher energy by $0.28\mathrm{eV},$ while I 3d peaks shift to lower energy by $1.81\mathrm{eV}.$ So, the amine groups in RBITC are active groups to interact with iodides in $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ precursor solutions. These interactions do not lead to the degradation of RBITC, as shown in Figure S3 (Supporting Information), where the color of the precursor solutions with RBITC remains to be red even after storing for 20 days in ambient conditions. Also, thermal treatment cannot severely degrade RBITC, as shown by the optical images of RBITC films on FTO substrates and the corresponding microscopic optical images in Figure S4 (Supporting Information).
To quantitatively characterize the degradation of pure RBITC on thermal treatment further, the annealing time dependent PL spectra were measured and shown in Figure S5a (Supporting Information). In the $15~\mathrm{min}$ annealing process, the intensity decreases fast in the initial $5~\mathrm{min}$ and is stable afterward, which shows the thermal stability of RBITC in current annealing conditions. The thermogravimetric curve of RBITC shows that the decomposition of RBITC occur at ${\approx}310^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure S6, Supporting Information), which is much higher than the annealing temperature. The contact of RBITC with perovskite induces the quenching of the perovskite PL probably due to charge transfer between $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ crystals and RBITC, as shown in Figure S5b (Supporting Information). These results show that RBITC itself is stable in the annealing condition. However, in the formation of perovskite film incorporating with RBITC (Figure 1a), the PL emission from RBITC decreases fast as the annealing process proceeding, as shown in Figure 1b, which indicates that the RBITC cations degrade. Figure 1d shows the PL spectra of perovskite films vs. annealing time, which indicates that after annealing for $5\mathrm{{min}}$ , the
PL from RBITC has been mostly quenched. After 15 min annealing, the PL from RBITC can be considered to disappear completely, as shown in the inset of Figure 1d. To further confirm the degradation of RBITC in the annealing process, the time of flight secondary ion mass spectrometry (TOF-SIMS) was performed. In the overall survey, no RBITC signals were observed (Figures S7 and S8, Supporting Information). In the TOF-SIMS spectra, large amount of ethylammonium cations $(\mathrm{EA^{+}})$ were found to exist in the film, which is evidenced by the distribution of $\mathrm{EA^{+}}$ , as shown in Figure 1e,f.
The observed degradation of RBITC can be considered as a thermal-photon catalytic degradation by the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathsf{P}\mathsf{b}\mathsf{I}_{3}$ nanocrystals created during annealing. In the initial annealing process, enormous $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ NCs nucleates as evidenced by the enhanced PL emission after 5 min annealing, as shown in Figure 1d. The surface morphological evolution confirms an Ostwald ripening process occurs during annealing (Figure 1c). The $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ NCs are efficient catalysts, which are highly active in absorption of photons and may transfer the absorbed photon energy from perovskite NCs to RBITC cations via the interaction between RBITC and perovskite NCs as described previously.[39,40] The excited RBITC may activate the water/oxygen molecules solved in the precursor solution and generate highly active oxidative species, such as $\mathrm{{OH/O^{2-}}}$ free radicals. These free radicals can efficiently destruct the RBITC into parts, as described in Figure 1g. From the PL evolution with the annealing time, just after 5 min annealing, the PL from RBITC has nearly disappeared completely, which indicates the superior catalytic ability of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ NCs. The main residue organic components are $\mathrm{EA}^{+}$ , $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ , BA, and some smaller inorganic components, such as carbon dioxide and water. The smaller inorganic components easily evaporate at the annealing temperature. The $\mathrm{EA}^{+}$ cations attached on perovskite NCs can efficiently passivate the surface defects. The DFT calculated coordination bond energy of $\mathrm{SCN^{-}}$ is much larger than that of DMSO (Figure S9, Supporting Information). So, the departed $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ can strongly interact with the unoccupied 6p orbitals of Pb atoms until they captured a proton to be released, where the coordination bond energy of SCNH is lowered greatly. It is interesting to note that there exist trace $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ anions at the boundary between perovskite film and substrate, as shown in Figure 1e. Though BA molecules are not found in TOF-SIMS spectra, they are considered as a major degradation product of RBITC.[41] BA molecules have a low coordination interaction energy with $\mathrm{{Pb}}$ (Figure S9, Supporting Information) and low sublimation temperature $(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , which leads to their release from perovskite film upon annealing. However, their existence is evidenced by the accelerated crystallization process of perovskite film. It is shown that the carboxylic acids play a role of anti-solvent, as shown in Figure S10 (Supporting Information), because the solubility of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ is decreased as the involvement of the carboxylic acid (Figure S11, Supporting Information). For RBITC addition, an accelerated crystallization process was also observed (Figure S12, Supporting Information). The more RBITC were added, the faster the crystallization of the perovskite film. Especially, when the samples were treated in dark condition, the fastest crystallization process was observed because all the RBITC were involved in the crystallization process. While if more photons were imposed on the samples, the crystallization of the perovskite film was deterred because of the evaporation of the degradation product of BA. These phenomena proved that the degradation of RBITC in the annealing process is a thermal-photon catalysis process.
Based on the above experimental results, the crystallization process of the perovskite film can be described in Figure 1h. The decomposition products of $\mathrm{EA^{+}}$ , $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ , and BA can be utilized to regulate the crystallization process. Figure $2\mathrm{a-k}$ show the SEM images of perovskite films with/without RBITC addition annealed in three conditions, i.e., dark, ambient light and illuminated by a LED light source with a power intensity of $0.339\mathrm{~mw~cm^{-2}}$ . The spectral profile of the LED light source is shown in Figure S13 (Supporting Information). Addition of RBITC in dark results in the decrease of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{P}{\mathsf{b}}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ crystal size (Figure 2a,e and 2i), because the large RBITC cations tend to prevent the merge of the small perovskite crystals. When ambient light was imposed during annealing, the large RBITC cations decomposed into small molecules/ions, which are easy to be removed during the merging of the small perovskite crystals. So we observed the increase of crystal size upon illumination as shown in Figure 2a,b, 2e,f. When under high flux of light illumination, the perovskite crystals may be excited in active states and the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ crystals were destructed and the quality of the perovskite film decreased, as shown in Figure 2g. After optimization, we found that in ambient condition, $0.5~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ is the optimized concentration for RBITC additive, where the surface roughness is the lowest (Figure S14, Supporting Information) and the PL intensity is the highest (Figure S15, Supporting Information). The perovskite with $0.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ RBITC additive under ambient condition is named as RBITC-ambient sample. For LED light illuminated samples, $1.0~\mathrm{{mg}\ m L^{-1}}$ is the optimized concentration for RBITC additive, as shown in Figure S16 (Supporting Information). The perovskite with $1.0~\mathrm{{mg}~\mathrm{{mL^{-1}}}}$ RBITC additive under LED light illumination is named as RBITC-illuminated sample. For comparison, we take the perovskite films made in ambient condition without RBITC doping as the control sample. The statistical results of the crystal size for control and optimized samples are shown in Figure 2d,h, and l. The statistical results show that the uniformity of the crystal size increases after annealing in ambient condition, and increases further under LED light illumination condition, though the average crystal size decreases slightly. The enhancement of the uniformity of the crystal size indicates the effective regulation by the decomposition products on the formation of perovskite film. Besides, the density of the pinholes decreases dramatically upon RBITC addition and illumination, by comparing Figure 2b,f and k. The decrease of the density of pinholes is beneficial to the enhancement of photovoltaic performance and the device stability.
The crystalline structure of all the perovskite films is 𝛾- $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ , according to the X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectra shown in Figure 2m. It is observed that the XRD peak intensity of sample with $1.0\mathrm{{mg}\mathrm{{mL}^{-1}}}$ RBITC addition under LED light illumination are the highest (Figure S17, Supporting Information), indicating their highest crystalline quality. The high quality is also confirmed by that the highest PL intensity (Figure 2n) and longest carrier lifetime (Figure 2o and Table S2, Supporting Information). Consistently, the trap density measured by space charge limited current (SCLC) method also shows that the trap density of optimized sample is the lowest $(6.87\times10^{15}~\mathrm{{cm}^{-3}}.$ ), as shown in Figure $2\mathrm{p}$ .
Figure 3a–c show the cross-sectional SEM images of the control, RBITC-ambient and RBITC-illuminated samples. Compared with control, RBITC-ambient samples, RBITC-illuminated sample exhibits a smoother surface and denser structure. The smooth surface is also confirmed by AFM measurement (Figure 3d–f). In addition, the surface of RBITC-illuminated sample is more hydrophobic (insets in Figure 3d–f), which is conducive to environmental stability of the fabricated PSCs.
Based on the optimization of the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ perovskite film, PSC devices were fabricated to study the photovoltaic (PV) performance. Figure 4a shows the typical PSC device structure.
UV photoemission spectroscopy (UPS) was performed to evaluate the band structure of perovskite film, as shown in Figure S18 and Table S3 (Supporting Information). The CBM and VBM was obtained for control and RBITC optimized samples, respectively, as shown in Figure 4b. The band gap is ${\approx}1.69\ \mathrm{eV}.$ which was extracted by the optical absorption spectra (Figure S19, Supporting Information). Compared with control device, the band energies of the RBITC-illuminated device were elevated by $0.3\mathrm{eV},$ so the energy band offset between VBM of perovskite film and the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) of Spiro-OMeTAD is reduced from $0.56\ \mathrm{eV}$ of the control to $0.26\mathrm{eV}$ of the RBITCilluminated, which facilitates the hole transportation from perovskite to Spiro-OMeTAD. Meanwhile, the energy band offset between CBM of perovskite film and CBM of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is increased from $-0.03$ to $0.33\ \mathrm{eV}.$ Though this increase may reduce the electron transport efficiency, a balanced charge transport of both electrons and holes is achieved, which benefit the improvement of PV performance.
The PV parameters, i.e. $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ , short-circuit current density $(J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}})$ , FF, and PCE, were extracted from the current density-voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves, as shown in Figure 4c. Compared with control device, $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF of RBITC-ambient device are greatly enhanced from 1.19 to $1.24\mathrm{V}$ and $76.99\%$ to $81.78\%$ , respectively, the high $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ indicates the low $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ loss. For RBITC-illuminated device, $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ increases further from 20.00 to $20.65\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , and FF from $81.78\%$ to $82.44\%$ , and a highest PCE of $20.95\%$ was achieved. A consistent trend for the PV parameters from the statistical data (Figure S20, Supporting Information) is also obtained. It is noted that for RBITC-illuminated devices, the dispersions of $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ , FF, $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ are the smallest, which indicates the highly uniformity and reproducibility of the PSC devices fabricated under illumination with RBITC doping.
The validity of $\dot{J}_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ was confirmed by the external quantum efficiency (EQE) measurement (Figure 4d). The integrated current density of control, RBTIC-ambient, and RBITC-illuminated devices are 19.71, 19.95, and $20.51\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, which agree well with the values extracted from J–V curves in Figure 4c. In the maximum power point (MPP) measurement, the control device gives a steady–state output PCE of $18.11\%$ under an operational voltage of $0.98\ \mathrm{V},$ as shown in Figure 4e. RBITCambient device gives an improved steady–state output PCE $20.36\%\textcircled{a}1.04\mathrm{V}$ . RBITC-illuminated device shows a further improved steady–state output PCE $20.73\%@1.04\mathrm{~V}.$ The MPP results are consistent with the parameters extracted from $J{-}V$ curves.
Capacitance-voltage (C-V) measurements were performed to obtain the build-in potentials $(V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ by using Mott-Schottky method.[42] $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ was obtained by the intercept of the linear region with the voltage axis in Mott–Schottky plot in Figure 4f. With RBITC addition, $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ was improved from 1.09 to 1.21 V for RBITCambient device and $1.20\mathrm{V}$ for RBITC-illuminated device, respectively. The enhanced $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ values for RBITC-ambient and RBITCilluminated devices can provide more efficient charge separation and collection in comparison with that of control devices and are well matched with the enhanced $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ extracted from $J{-}V$ curves. The incident light intensity dependent $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ was measured to clarify the recombination mechanism. The ideality factor is defined as the slope of the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ versus light intensity curve, as shown in Figure ${4\mathrm{g}}$ . The ideality factor decreases from $1.59\mathrm{~k_{\mathrm{B}}T/q}$ for control devices to $1.36~\mathrm{k_{B}T/q}$ for RBITC-ambient devices and $1.34\mathrm{k_{B}T/q}$ for RBITC-illuminated devices, respectively, where $\mathrm{k_{B}}$ is Boltzmann constant, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\DeltaT}}$ is the absolute temperature, and $\mathsf{q}$ is the elemental charge. The ideality factor of RBITC-illuminated devices is closer to $1\mathrm{k_{B}T/q}$ , which reveals a decrease in trapassisted recombination and is consistent with the density of the trap states measured by SCLC method (Figure 2p). Dark current is also greatly reduced by RBITC addition and reduced further by illumination as shown in Figure 4h. As shown in Figure 4i and Table S4, electric impedance spectroscopy measurements of PSC devices were performed in dark at a bias of 0.98 V, the equivalent circuit is shown in the inset. Significantly, the recombination resistance $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ increases from $509.3\Omega$ for control devices to $1417\Omega$ for RBITC-ambient devices and $4461\Omega$ for RBITC-illuminated devices, respectively. The nearly one order enhanced $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ indicates the much better passivation of defects in the perovskite film of RBITC-illuminated devices by more $\mathrm{EA}^{+}$ cations than both the control and RBITC-ambient devices.
The environmental stability of PSCs is an important factor in their wide usage. Figure 5a show the photographs of perovskite films stored in dry air along with time. It is clear after stored for $120\mathrm{h}$ , a large area of control sample has been converted to yellow phase, an apparent $\delta\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ peak appeared at ${\approx}10^{\circ}$ in the XRD pattern (Figure 5b). While for RBITC-ambient films, the major areas are kept in black phase after being stored for $120\mathrm{~h~}$ . For RBITC-illuminated films, the stability was enhanced further and most of the area are in black even after being stored for $120\mathrm{~h~}$ . The intensities of the $\delta\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C s P b I}_{3}$ peak are largely reduced. Accordingly, the stability of PSCs is also enhanced by RBITC addition (Figure 5c–e). For control devices, the PCE remains $75.12\%$ of the original value after being stored in $25\%$ relative humidity (RH) for $1000\mathrm{~h~}$ . For the RBITC-ambient devices, the PCE remains $89.14\%$ of the original value after being stored under the same conditions. For the RBITC-illuminated devices, the stability was enhanced further to remain $90.39\%$ of the original value after stored in $25\%$ RH for $1000\mathrm{h}$ . Thermal stability was tested by storing the PSC devices at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere. The PCE of the control devices decreases by $67.05\%$ after being stored for $500\mathrm{{h}}$ , while for RBITC-ambient devices, only $16.00\%$ of PCE was lost. For RBITC-illuminated devices, the PCE loss was further reduced to $13.98\%$ . In the MPP mode, the PCE drops only $6.4\%$ of the initial PCE after continuous illumination for $100\mathrm{{h}}$ , while it drops $71.3\%$ for the control devices. The enhancement of stability is consistent with the water contact angle results shown in Figure 3.
the defects on the $\mathrm{CsPbI}_{3}$ crystal boundaries. Due to the photon sensitivity of the catalytic degradation of RBITC, illumination can improve the quality of the perovskite films further. After optimization, a champion PCE of $20.95\%$ was achieved and the stability of the devices in $25\%$ RH in air, $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $\Nu_{2}$ , and continuous illumination was significantly improved. This work provides a new method for preparing high-efficiency perovskite solar cells by using photodecomposition materials as additive.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have demonstrated a strategy to perform perovskite film regulation and defect passivation utilizing a thermalphoton catalytic degradation of the large RBITC molecules. The products of the catalytic degradation are BA, $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ and $\mathrm{EA}^{+}$ . BA is helpful to initial nucleation of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\ensuremath{\mathrm{I}_{3}}$ crystal grains, $\mathsf{S C N^{-}}$ can deterred the crystallization process to obtain high quality of perovskite films, and $\mathrm{EA^{+}}$ can efficiently in situ passivate
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: Cesium iodide (CsI) $(\geq99.5\%)$ , hydrogen lead triiodide $(\mathsf{H P b}\mathsf{I}_{3})$ $(\geq99.5\%)$ and Spiro-OMeTAD were purchased from Xi’an Polymer Light Technology Corp. Titanium tetrachloride $(\mathsf{T i C l}_{4})$ $(\geq98.0\%)$ , N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) $(\geq99.9\%)$ , Rhodamine B isothiocyanate (RBITC), Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) $(\geq99.9\%)$ and benzoic acid $(\geq99.5\%)$ were purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. 4-tert-butylpyridine (t-BP), chlorobenzene (CB), and lithiumbis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl)imide salt (Li-TFSI) were purchased from
Sigma–Aldrich. Formic acid $(\geq99.9\%)$ was purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd.
Preparation of Solution: $\mathsf{H P b}\mathsf{I}_{3}$ $(0.4387\ g)$ and CsI $(0.7974\ g)$ were dissolved in a mixed solution of DMSO and DMF $7m L$ (the ratio, $\mathsf{V}/\mathsf{V}=$ 1.5:8.5) and further stirred for $20\textup{h}$ to get the pristine $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}$ perovskite precursor solution (0.745 M). The $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}$ perovskite precursor solutions with additives were obtained by adding Rhodamine B isothiocyanate with the concentration from 0.5 to $3\max\cdot2^{-7}$ . Spiro-OMeTAD powder $(90\mathsf{m g})$ was dissolved in $7m L$ chlorobenzene. Then, t-BP $(36~\upmu\up L)$ and Li-TFSI $(22\upmu\up L)$ were added into the above solution, and further stirred for $\mathsf{12h}$ .
Device Fabrication: FTO glass substrates were cleaned sequentially with Hellmanex aqueous solution and distilled water in an ultrasonic bath for $30{\mathrm{~}}{\mathrm{min}}$ . After that, the cleaned FTO substrate was treated with UVOzone for $10-75\mathrm{\min}$ , then immersed in the mixed solution of $200~\mathrm{mL}$ water and $5~\mathrm{mL}$ titanium tetrachloride at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $60~\mathrm{min}$ , and then immersed in ethanol. Before spin coating, the deposited $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ substrates were annealed at $200~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30{\mathrm{~}}{\mathrm{min}}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, they were put into UV-Ozone for $10\min$ . The perovskite precursor solutions without and with additives (RBITC) were spin-coated onto $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ coated FTO substrate at 1000 rpm for $\rceil0\ s$ and 3000 rpm for $40~\mathsf{s}$ to obtain $\mathsf{F T O}/\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ /perovskite substrate. The perovskite precursor film was annealed at $170^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $50\mathrm{\min}$ to crystallize. After the perovskite film was completely crystallized, $50~\upmu\up L$ Spiro-OMeTAD solution was spin-coated onto the perovskite films at 5000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ to obtain the FTO/ $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ /perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD substrate. Finally, $80\mathsf{n m A u}$ electrode was deposited on the spin-coated hole transport layer substrate to form a device with an effective area of $0.09\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ .
Device Characterization: XRD patterns were acquired on a D/MAX 2400 diffractometer with $\mathsf{C u K}\alpha$ radiation (1.5405 Å). The absorption spectra were collected using a UV–vis NIR spectroscopy (Perkin Elmer, Lambda 950). The XPS and UPS measurements were carried out using a photoelectron spectrometer (Thermo Scientific, ESCA Lab 250Xi system). The SEM images of the $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}$ films (without/with different concentration of RBITC) were obtained by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM, HITACHI, SU-8020). AFM images of $\mathsf{C s P b l}_{3}$ films (without/with different concentration of RBITC) were taken by BRUKER Atomic Force Microscope. The steady PL spectra were measured via using a FLS980 spectrometer (Edinburgh Instruments Ltd) and its excitation wavelength was $570~\mathsf{n m}$ . The transient fluorescence spectroscopy spectra (TRPL) were measured with a PicoQuant FluoTime 300. The J–V curves were obtained using a Keitheley 2400 source under simulated sunlight (AM 1.5G, SAN-EIELECTRIC XES-40S2-CE) with a scanning rate of $\bar{10\mathrm{~mV~}}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ . EQE tests were measured by a QTest Station 2000 ADI (Crowntech, Inc. USA), calibrated prior to testing with a standard silicon detector over a wavelength range of 500 to $850~\mathsf{n m}$ . EIS and $C-V$ results obtained using Solartron electrochemical workstation. TOF-SIMS were performed on a PHI nanoTOF II Time-ofFlight SIMS in both positive and negative modes. Mass spectroscopy was performed on an Ultra-high Performance Liquid Chromatography-Time-ofFlight Mass Spectrometry (Bruker Maxis Utimate 300hplc). The optical microscopic images were obtained on an Olympus DP22/DP27 microscope with a low pass filter. The LED light source power intensity was measured by Laser power meter from Ophir-Vega.
Density Functional Theory (DFT) Calculation: The calculations were performed using VASP code[43,44] in the frame work of the PBE approximation.[45] van der Waals interaction was treated by the Tkatchenko-Scheffler method.[46] The coordination bond energies were calculated by the energy difference between interacting molecule/ion with $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ and the sum of molecule/ion and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ . The individual energies of molecule/ion and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ were calculated in a volume of $20\times20\times20\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{3}$ , and a $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\sf~k~}}$ -mesh of $\mathsf{l}\times\mathsf{l}\times\mathsf{l}$ was used. The cutoff energy was set to $500~\mathrm{eV}$ and the convergence energy was set to ${\mathsf{10}}^{-6}{\mathsf{e V}}.$
Statistics Analysis: All statistics analyses were performed with Origin 2018. All the data keep two significant digits after the decimal points by the rounding-off method. The data obtained from SEM, AFM, UV–vis, PL,
TRPL, contact angles, XPS, UPS, $J{-}V,$ and EQE were the original data without normalized. The other data were obtained by transferring corresponding original data according to the calculation formula. Linear fittings were applied to SCLC (Figure 2p), $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ interception (Figure 4f), and $V_{\mathsf{O C}}$ versus light intensity plots (Figure $4\mathrm{g}$ ). The statistical distribution data of PCE, $J_{s c}$ , $V_{o c}$ and FF were got from 40 devices each. The size distribution of perovskite film was obtained by using Nano Measurer software.
